<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicente Cerna y Cerna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicente Cerna y Cerna (1815–1885) was a Guatemalan general and conservative politician who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> as president in 1865. He continued Carrera's alliance with the church and the conservative elite but faced growing liberal opposition, and he was overthrown in 1871 by the Liberal Revolution led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicente-cerna-y-cerna.jpg" alt="Vicente Cerna y Cerna" width="240" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presidencia de Guatemala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VicenteCerna_(oficial).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
